data: {"type":"token","content":"**Microsoft makes most of its money by selling cloud-computing capacity and AI services, subscriptions to workplace and business software, and software licenses.** It also earns revenue from gaming, advertising, devices, professional-network services, and technical support. Microsoft reports these activities in three main business segments. [1]\n\nFor its fiscal year ended **June 30, 2026**, the latest full year available as of **September 3, 2026**, Microsoft generated **$331.839 billion in revenue** and **$133.749 billion in net income**. [1]\n\n| Main business | FY2026 revenue | What customers pay for |\n|---|---:|---|\n| **Productivity and Business Processes** | **$139.996B** | Microsoft 365 and Copilot subscriptions, Office licenses, LinkedIn services and advertising, and Dynamics business software |\n| **Intelligent Cloud** | **$137.791B** | Azure computing, storage, databases and AI usage; GitHub; server software; support and consulting |\n| **More Personal Computing** | **$54.052B** | Windows licenses, Xbox games and subscriptions, Surface and accessories, and search advertising |\n| **Total** | **$331.839B** | - |\n\n### 1. Productivity and Business Processes\n\nThis is Microsoft\u2019s largest segment by FY2026 revenue. Its core products are Microsoft 365, LinkedIn and Dynamics. [1]\n\n- **Microsoft 365:** Businesses and consumers pay recurring subscriptions for applications and services such as Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams, OneDrive, security products and Microsoft 365 Copilot. Microsoft also still sells some on-premises Office and Windows Commercial licenses. [1]\n- **LinkedIn:** Revenue comes from recruiting and talent products, sales tools, premium memberships and advertising through Marketing Solutions. [1]\n- **Dynamics:** Companies pay for cloud and on-premises enterprise-resource-planning and customer-relationship-management software, including Dynamics 365, Power Apps and Power Automate. Revenue is influenced by licensed users, applications consumed and revenue per user. [1]\n\n### 2. Intelligent Cloud\n\nThis segment contains Azure and Microsoft\u2019s server, developer and enterprise-service businesses. [1]\n\n- **Azure:** Customers generally pay according to how much cloud infrastructure and platform capacity they consume, such as computing, networking, storage, databases, AI and application services. [1]\n- **Server and developer products:** Microsoft licenses SQL Server, Windows Server, Visual Studio, System Center and related access rights. GitHub provides both cloud and on-premises developer tools, including AI-assisted offerings. [1]\n- **Enterprise services:** Microsoft charges for support, consulting, implementation assistance, training and certification. [1]\n\nThis was Microsoft\u2019s fastest-growing major segment in FY2026: revenue rose **30%**, with Azure and other cloud-services revenue increasing **41%**. Microsoft reported **$214.4 billion of Microsoft Cloud revenue**, a broader measure spanning several segments. [1]\n\n### 3. More Personal Computing\n\nThis segment combines Microsoft\u2019s consumer-facing and PC-related businesses. [1]\n\n- **Windows:** PC manufacturers pay to license and preinstall Windows on computers; organizations also purchase commercial Windows licenses. [1]\n- **Xbox:** Revenue comes from game and in-game-content sales, Xbox Game Pass and other subscriptions, advertising, digital stores, cloud gaming and console hardware. [1]\n- **Search advertising:** Advertisers pay Microsoft to place search, native and display ads across services including Bing, Copilot, Edge and affiliated properties. [1]\n- **Devices:** Microsoft sells Surface computers and PC accessories. [1]\n\n### The simple picture\n\nMicrosoft now has a mixed model:\n\n1. **Subscriptions** for Microsoft 365, Copilot, Dynamics, LinkedIn and Xbox services.\n2. **Usage-based charges** for Azure cloud and AI workloads.\n3. **Software licensing** for Windows, Office and server products.\n4. **Advertising** through search, LinkedIn and gaming.\n5. **Transaction and content sales** through games, digital stores and related services.\n6. **Hardware sales and professional services** from Surface, Xbox consoles, accessories, consulting and support. [1]\n\nThe economic center of the company is therefore **enterprise cloud infrastructure plus recurring business-software subscriptions**, although Windows, gaming and advertising remain sizable businesses. In FY2026, Productivity and Business Processes and Intelligent Cloud together generated **$277.787 billion**, or **83.7%** of Microsoft\u2019s total revenue. [1]"}

data: {"type":"final","payload":{"artifact_references":[],"stage_outcome":"ready_to_respond","assistant_response":"**Microsoft makes most of its money by selling cloud-computing capacity and AI services, subscriptions to workplace and business software, and software licenses.** It also earns revenue from gaming, advertising, devices, professional-network services, and technical support. Microsoft reports these activities in three main business segments. [1]\n\nFor its fiscal year ended **June 30, 2026**, the latest full year available as of **September 3, 2026**, Microsoft generated **$331.839 billion in revenue** and **$133.749 billion in net income**. [1]\n\n| Main business | FY2026 revenue | What customers pay for |\n|---|---:|---|\n| **Productivity and Business Processes** | **$139.996B** | Microsoft 365 and Copilot subscriptions, Office licenses, LinkedIn services and advertising, and Dynamics business software |\n| **Intelligent Cloud** | **$137.791B** | Azure computing, storage, databases and AI usage; GitHub; server software; support and consulting |\n| **More Personal Computing** | **$54.052B** | Windows licenses, Xbox games and subscriptions, Surface and accessories, and search advertising |\n| **Total** | **$331.839B** | - |\n\n### 1. Productivity and Business Processes\n\nThis is Microsoft\u2019s largest segment by FY2026 revenue. Its core products are Microsoft 365, LinkedIn and Dynamics. [1]\n\n- **Microsoft 365:** Businesses and consumers pay recurring subscriptions for applications and services such as Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams, OneDrive, security products and Microsoft 365 Copilot. Microsoft also still sells some on-premises Office and Windows Commercial licenses. [1]\n- **LinkedIn:** Revenue comes from recruiting and talent products, sales tools, premium memberships and advertising through Marketing Solutions. [1]\n- **Dynamics:** Companies pay for cloud and on-premises enterprise-resource-planning and customer-relationship-management software, including Dynamics 365, Power Apps and Power Automate. Revenue is influenced by licensed users, applications consumed and revenue per user. [1]\n\n### 2. Intelligent Cloud\n\nThis segment contains Azure and Microsoft\u2019s server, developer and enterprise-service businesses. [1]\n\n- **Azure:** Customers generally pay according to how much cloud infrastructure and platform capacity they consume, such as computing, networking, storage, databases, AI and application services. [1]\n- **Server and developer products:** Microsoft licenses SQL Server, Windows Server, Visual Studio, System Center and related access rights. GitHub provides both cloud and on-premises developer tools, including AI-assisted offerings. [1]\n- **Enterprise services:** Microsoft charges for support, consulting, implementation assistance, training and certification. [1]\n\nThis was Microsoft\u2019s fastest-growing major segment in FY2026: revenue rose **30%**, with Azure and other cloud-services revenue increasing **41%**. Microsoft reported **$214.4 billion of Microsoft Cloud revenue**, a broader measure spanning several segments. [1]\n\n### 3. More Personal Computing\n\nThis segment combines Microsoft\u2019s consumer-facing and PC-related businesses. [1]\n\n- **Windows:** PC manufacturers pay to license and preinstall Windows on computers; organizations also purchase commercial Windows licenses. [1]\n- **Xbox:** Revenue comes from game and in-game-content sales, Xbox Game Pass and other subscriptions, advertising, digital stores, cloud gaming and console hardware. [1]\n- **Search advertising:** Advertisers pay Microsoft to place search, native and display ads across services including Bing, Copilot, Edge and affiliated properties. [1]\n- **Devices:** Microsoft sells Surface computers and PC accessories. [1]\n\n### The simple picture\n\nMicrosoft now has a mixed model:\n\n1. **Subscriptions** for Microsoft 365, Copilot, Dynamics, LinkedIn and Xbox services.\n2. **Usage-based charges** for Azure cloud and AI workloads.\n3. **Software licensing** for Windows, Office and server products.\n4. **Advertising** through search, LinkedIn and gaming.\n5. **Transaction and content sales** through games, digital stores and related services.\n6. **Hardware sales and professional services** from Surface, Xbox consoles, accessories, consulting and support. [1]\n\nThe economic center of the company is therefore **enterprise cloud infrastructure plus recurring business-software subscriptions**, although Windows, gaming and advertising remain sizable businesses. In FY2026, Productivity and Business Processes and Intelligent Cloud together generated **$277.787 billion**, or **83.7%** of Microsoft\u2019s total revenue. [1]","next_experiments":{"version":"argus_next_experiments/v1","rows":[{"kind":"research_test_single","label":"Test Microsoft (MSFT) over the last 3 years","label_parts":[{"type":"text","value":"Test "},{"type":"text","value":"Microsoft"},{"type":"ticker","value":"MSFT"},{"type":"text","value":" over the last 3 years"}],"label_key":"chat.next_experiments.labels.research_dynamic","send_text":"Test buying and holding MSFT over the last three years"}]},"research":{"schema_version":"argus_research/v1","capability_class":"balanced_lookup","shape":"balanced","sources":[{"title":"Form 10-K for Microsoft Corp filed 07/29/2026","domain":"microsoft.gcs-web.com","url":"https://microsoft.gcs-web.com/static-files/8a9beeed-0e9e-48e6-b4fb-593d558e2693","source_date":"2026-07-29"},{"title":"Microsoft FY2026 Earnings: The $133B Profit Machine Now ...","domain":"beancount.io","url":"https://beancount.io/blog/2026/07/29/microsoft-fy2026-earnings-analysis","source_date":"2026-07-29"},{"title":"FY26 Q2 - Segment Results - Investor Relations","domain":"www.microsoft.com","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/investor/earnings/fy-2026-q2/segment-revenues","source_date":"2026-08-26"},{"title":"MICROSOFT CORP annual report, FY2026 (NASDAQ:MSFT) | MetaTrader","domain":"www.m